### Page 6 of 18

```text
Letter of Intent
Purchase of Danielmark’s Brewing Company, Inc.
Owners of Danielmark’s Brewing Company, Inc.
209 E. 1gth Street,
Cheyenne, WY 82001
January 7, 2021
Dear Daniel, Kim, Paul and Beth,
This letter is to inform you that Ryan Willson, Kevin Tighe, and Richard Hart (through an entity to be formed by them)
(the “Buyer”) have intent to purchase all or substantially all of the assets of Danielmark’s Brewing Company, Inc. (the
“Company”) for $900K, which includes without limitation all real estate, business assets, inventory, and intellectual
property owned by the Company. This purchase is contingent upon approval and receipt of a bank loan, approval and
receipt of an SBA loan, and the seller’s providing a seller’s note in the amount and terms indicated below.
The agreement for a $900K sale price for the assets of the Company as well as the seller’s note terms were reached
using non-appraised values for the business, the real property, the furniture/equipment, and the inventory. These non-
appraised values were provided by the sellers and utilized by lending entities to form preliminary loan estimates. These
preliminary loan estimates were subtracted from the agreed upon sale price of $900K to determine an estimated seller’s
carry of $350K.
After appraised values are determined for the business, the real property, the furniture/equipment, and the inventory,
and if the difference between the $900K sale price and the combined appraised values is less than $50K, the seller’s
note will increase in size to cover any amount not covered by the reduced anticipated loan values caused by the lower
appraisals/available collateral. The seller’s note, as agreed upon between sellers and buyers, is currently estimated to be
$350K. The seller’s note will have an interest rate of 3.5%, will feature a 20-year amortization with a 10-year balloon,
and be paid in monthly installments. The buyers reserve the right to pay off the seller’s note early and without penalty,
and the buyers will provide extra payments towards the seller’s note when financially feasible to do so.
After appraised values are determined for the business, the real property, the furniture/equipment, and the inventory,
and if the difference between the $900K sale price and the combined appraised values is greater than $50K, the buyers
(Ryan Willson, Kevin Tighe, and Richard Hart) reserve the right to renegotiate the sale price to a value congruent to the
appraised values. If a new negotiated price cannot be reached, the buyers reserve the right to cancel the transaction
entirely.
The seller’s note will be subordinate to the loans provided by the lender(s). Also, all accounts payable must be paid in
full prior to the company’s transition to new ownership. No debt will transfer to or liabilities assumed by the new
ownership.
These estimated sale/acquisition related expenses will be paid evenly by all 5 parties at 20% each:
•
Accountant Business Valuation: $4000
•
Bank Loan Origination Fee: $3000
•
SBA/CDC Processing Fee: $3000
•
Legal Closing Fee: $2500
[Type here]
```

### Page 7 of 18

```text
These 5 parties agree to splitting the indicated sale/acquisition related expenses evenly:
•
Party 1: Paul Warntz, Beth Warntz
•
Party 2: Daniel Combs, Kim Combs
•
Party 3: Ryan Willson
•
Party 4: Kevin Tighe
Party 5: Richard Hart
With the approximate total of estimated fees to be $12500, each party is responsible for contributing roughly $2500
towards these fees. All indicated expenses could change, but the 20% payment sharing agreement will remain in place.
All other fees incurred will not be split between parties unless a separate agreement is reached for a specific expense
not listed in this agreement. If a new negotiation for sale price must occur due to lower-than-expected appraisals, and if
the sale is terminated due to inability to reach a new sale price agreement, only the $4000 Accountant Business
Valuation Fee would apply and still must be paid in full at 20% by all parties indicated.
In consideration of the expenses to be incurred by the Buyer, the Company and each of its shareholders agree as
follows:
(a) Throughout the period that begins on the date of this letter and ends on May 15, 2022 (the “Exclusivity Period”), the
Company will not, and will cause each of its shareholders, representatives and agents not to, directly or indirectly, solicit,
initiate, seek or encourage any inquiry, proposal or offer from, furnish any information to, or participate in any
discussions or negotiations with, any person (otherthan Buyer or any person on Buyer’s behalf) regarding any acquisition
of the Company or any of its capital stock, assets or business, in whole or in part, except sales of inventory in the ordinary
course of the Company’s business. The Company will, and will cause each of its shareholders, representatives and agents
to, immediately terminate all such discussions or negotiations that may be in progress as of the date hereof.
(b) Throughout the Exclusivity Period, the Company will give to Buyer full and free access to the Company and its
personnel, properties, contracts, books and records and all other documents and data.
(c) Throughout the Exclusivity Period, the Company will operate its business in the ordinary course, consistent with past
practices, and refrain from any extraordinary transaction.
Parties agreeing to the above business/real property sale terms, seller’s note terms, and expense sharing terms, please
